(test_uuid,'Reading · Daily and Academic','choice','What is the main purpose of the passage?','Many flowering plants depend on animals to transfer pollen between flowers. Bees are well-known pollinators, but they are not the only ones. In tropical forests, bats visit flowers that open at night. These flowers often have pale colors, strong odors, and large amounts of nectar—features that help nocturnal animals locate them. When a bat pushes its head into a flower to drink, pollen attaches to its fur and may be carried to another plant.

Bat pollination is especially important where flowering plants are widely separated. Bats can travel farther than many insects and therefore connect distant plant populations. This movement supports genetic diversity, which may help plants respond to disease or environmental change. Some economically useful plants, including certain fruits, also benefit from bat visits.

However, the relationship is vulnerable. Forest clearing can remove both feeding sites and safe places where bats rest during the day. Artificial lighting may also change when bats leave their roosts or which routes they use. Conservation programs increasingly protect networks of habitat rather than isolated trees. The goal is to preserve a sequence of feeding and resting locations that mobile pollinators can use across the landscape.',null,'["To compare the diets of bats and bees", "To explain the role of bats in pollination and why habitat networks matter", "To argue that artificial light improves fruit production", "To describe how insects find flowers at night"]'::jsonb,'"To explain the role of bats in pollination and why habitat networks matter"'::jsonb,null,'published'::public.content_status,18,'original','User-supplied TOEFL iBT 2026–Aligned Classroom Mock Form A; README declares all items original and not official ETS items.',now()),
