(test_uuid,'Listening · Classroom Practice','choice','What is the talk mainly about?',null,'A seed contains a young plant, but it does not always begin growing immediately. Many seeds enter a state called dormancy. During dormancy, growth is temporarily suspended, even though the seed is alive. This delay is an adaptation: it prevents the plant from beginning its life when temperature or water conditions are unfavorable. Different species use different environmental signals to end dormancy. Some seeds need a period of cold. Others respond to changes in light or moisture. In certain fire-prone ecosystems, heat or chemicals in smoke can weaken a seed coat. Once water can pass through that coat, growth begins. Fire sounds destructive, but for these plants it also creates open space and releases nutrients. Desert plants provide another example. Their seeds may remain inactive through long dry periods. After heavy rain, many seeds can germinate within a short time. As a result, young plants begin growth when water is temporarily abundant. Dormancy, then, is not simply inactivity. It is a timing system that helps match growth with opportunity.','["Why some seeds remain inactive until conditions improve", "How farmers transport seeds over long distances", "Why desert plants have shallow roots", "How insects damage stored seeds"]'::jsonb,'"Why some seeds remain inactive until conditions improve"'::jsonb,null,'published'::public.content_status,40,'original','User-supplied TOEFL iBT 2026–Aligned Classroom Mock Form A; README declares all items original and not official ETS items.',now()),
